{
  // Dependency-vulnerability gate for CI (replaces the bare `npm audit --audit-level=high`,
  // which has no way to record a reasoned exception).
  //
  // The gate stays sharp: anything high/critical that is NOT listed below fails the build.
  // `allowlist` is per-advisory (GHSA id), never per-severity — we do not lower the level.
  //
  // Adding an entry requires, in this file: (1) the advisory id, (2) why it does not apply to
  // THIS codebase, measured — not assumed, (3) an expiry date, (4) what removes the need for it.
  // An expired entry is a task, not a renewal: re-check it, do not extend it by reflex.
  //
  // Prefer the fix. Of the 11 advisories present on 2026-07-29, 8 were removed by upgrading
  // (brace-expansion@5 5.0.8, postcss 8.5.24, react-router-dom 7.18.1) — see
  // docs/development/dependency-audit.md. The two entries below have no fix we can adopt.
  "$schema": "https://github.com/IBM/audit-ci/raw/main/docs/schema.json",
  "moderate": false,
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "allowlist": [
    // GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router "RSC Mode CSRF Bypass Allows Action Execution Before
    // 400 Response" (high). Vulnerable range 7.12.0–8.2.0; there is NO fix in the 7.x line
    // (react-router-dom ends at 7.18.1; the fix lands in react-router v8 >= 8.2.1, a different
    // package and a breaking upgrade). npm's own `fixAvailable` proposes react-router-dom
    // 7.11.0 — that is a *downgrade* below the four advisories 7.18.1 just fixed, not a fix.
    //
    // Why it does not apply here (measured 2026-07-29, in this tree — not copied from a
    // sibling): the Deal console is a **static SPA built by Vite**. `src/app/router.tsx` is
    // 64 lines of `createBrowserRouter` with **element-only routes** (13 routes, all
    // `{ path, element }`) — no RSC mode, no server components, no route `action`/`loader`
    // executing on a server, and no `@react-router/dev` runtime. Checked by:
    //   - grep for route-level `action:` / `loader:` keys under src/ → 0
    //     (the 3 textual matches are `DealActivity.action`, a domain field)
    //   - grep for `@react-router/dev` / `react-router/rsc` / `createStaticHandler` /
    //     `createStaticRouter` / `ServerRouter` / `StaticRouter` under src/ → 0
    //   - `node_modules/@react-router` → absent
    // The advisory's attack path (a server executing a route action before returning 400)
    // has no counterpart in a client-only bundle.
    //
    // Expiry: 2026-08-31. Removed by the react-router v8 migration wave (bundled with the
    // NENE2 RR8 re-evaluation). If that wave slips past the expiry, the entry must be
    // re-argued in a PR — not silently extended.
    "GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2",

    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg — brace-expansion "DoS via unbounded expansion length" (high).
    // Vulnerable range <=5.0.7, i.e. every 1.x and 2.x release as well; the fix exists only
    // as 5.0.8.
    //
    // We take 5.0.8 wherever it can be taken — `overrides` carries `"brace-expansion@5":
    // "^5.0.8"`. It CANNOT be taken everywhere: brace-expansion changed its entry point
    // between v2 and v5 (`module.exports = expand` → `module.exports = { expand, ... }`),
    // and minimatch@3 / minimatch@5 call the module's default export directly. Forcing v5 on
    // them throws `TypeError: expand is not a function` on any brace pattern — measured
    // 2026-07-29, and the reason this repo briefly shipped a broken eslint/codegen toolchain.
    // So minimatch@3 keeps brace-expansion 1.1.16 and minimatch@5 keeps 2.1.3, both in range.
    //
    // Why it does not apply here (measured 2026-07-29): every remaining copy is **dev-only
    // tooling that never reaches a user**. The packages pulling them in are
    // eslint-plugin-import, eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y (via @hideyukimori/nene2-standards) and
    // @redocly/openapi-core (via openapi-typescript) — all devDependencies. Verified by:
    //   - `npm ls brace-expansion minimatch --omit=dev --all` → "(empty)": absent from the
    //     production dependency tree entirely
    //   - `npm run build` then grepping dist/ for `brace-expansion` / `braceExpand` → 0 files
    // The advisory's impact (OOM crash from an adversarial brace pattern) would require an
    // attacker-supplied glob to our lint/codegen commands, which are run by us on our own
    // source, never on untrusted input, and never at runtime.
    //
    // A regression guard keeps this honest: tests/toolchain/brace-expansion-override.test.ts
    // resolves every installed minimatch and runs a brace pattern through it, so a future
    // override that reintroduces the break fails CI instead of passing quietly.
    //
    // Expiry: 2026-08-31. Removed when minimatch@3/@5 leave the tree — i.e. when
    // eslint-plugin-import and @redocly/openapi-core ship on minimatch@10 (which imports
    // `{ expand }` and is already on 5.0.8 here). Re-check at expiry; do not extend by reflex.
    "GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg",

    // GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 — brace-expansion, a SECOND unbounded-expansion advisory (high),
    // published after 2026-07-31 and distinct from GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg above. It matters
    // because its vulnerable range is wider — `<=1.1.17 || 2.0.0-2.1.3 || 4.0.0-5.0.8` — so
    // it covers the 5.0.8 that the `overrides` entry deliberately pins to dodge the first
    // advisory. Every installed copy is in range (measured 2026-08-04: 1.1.16 ×2, 2.1.3 ×1,
    // 5.0.8 ×1).
    //
    // ⚠️ A PARTIAL fix exists and is NOT taken here — stated plainly rather than claiming
    // there is none. brace-expansion 5.0.9 is published and fixes the v5 copy, and the
    // existing `"brace-expansion@5": "^5.0.8"` override would accept it on a lockfile
    // refresh. It is deferred on purpose: (a) the 1.1.16 / 2.1.3 copies stay vulnerable
    // regardless, because minimatch@3 / minimatch@5 call the module's default export and
    // throw on the v5 entry point (the breakage documented above), so the allowlist entry
    // is needed either way; (b) the lockfile bump belongs to the fleet-wide dependency
    // wave, not to a CSS drain PR. hub routed that as the cross-repo work order (board
    // 2026-08-05). This PR touches the allowlist only — no lockfile, no overrides.
    //
    // Why it does not apply here (measured 2026-08-04, in this tree):
    //   - `npm ls brace-expansion --omit=dev --all` → "(empty)" — absent from the
    //     production dependency tree entirely
    //   - `grep -rl 'brace-expansion|braceExpand' dist/` after `npm run build` → 0 files
    // Same reasoning as GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg: the impact is an OOM crash from an adversarial
    // brace pattern, which would require attacker-supplied globs to our lint/codegen
    // commands. Those run on our own source, never on untrusted input, never at runtime.
    //
    // Expiry: 2026-08-31 (aligned with the two entries above). Removed when minimatch@3/@5
    // leave the tree and the v5 copy reaches >=5.0.9. Re-check at expiry; do not extend by
    // reflex.
    "GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895",

    // GHSA-7p8r-x3mc-p8w7 — fast-uri (high), published after 2026-07-31. Vulnerable range
    // 3.0.0-3.1.4; installed copy is exactly one, fast-uri@3.1.4.
    //
    // No fix we can adopt. fast-uri 4.1.2 is published and out of range, but the only thing
    // pulling fast-uri here is ajv@8.20.0, which declares `"fast-uri": "^3.0.1"` (measured
    // in node_modules/table/node_modules/ajv/package.json). Overriding it to 4.x forces a
    // major past the range its consumer accepts — the same class of mistake as npm's
    // proposed react-router-dom "fix" above, and not something to do on a style PR.
    //
    // Why it does not apply here (measured 2026-08-04, in this tree): the single path is
    // dev-only tooling —
    //   `stylelint@16.26.1 -> table@6.9.0 -> ajv@8.20.0 -> fast-uri@3.1.4`
    // and stylelint is a devDependency (`dependencies` → false, `devDependencies` → true).
    // Confirmed by:
    //   - `npm ls fast-uri --omit=dev --all` → "(empty)"
    //   - `grep -rl 'fast-uri|fastUri' dist/` after `npm run build` → 0 files
    // fast-uri parses URIs for ajv's JSON-Schema `format` keyword; the only schemas it sees
    // are stylelint's own config schemas, authored by us, never user input.
    //
    // Expiry: 2026-08-31 (aligned with the entries above). Removed when stylelint ships an
    // ajv that accepts fast-uri >=4 (or ajv backports to 3.x). Re-check at expiry.
    "GHSA-7p8r-x3mc-p8w7",
  ],
}
